Sure! I was just working in two `psql` windows, and I did:

```sql
-- CREATE TABLE foo (a integer); CREATE TABLE bar (a integer);

BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
UPDATE foo SET a = 2;
                                            BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
                                            SAVEPOINT x;
                                            UPDATE foo SET a = 3;
                                            -- blocks until:
COMMIT;
                                            -- ERROR
                                            ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT x;
                                            INSERT INTO bar VALUES (99);
                                            COMMIT;
```

Because the outer transaction hasn't been restarted, any second attempt to update `foo` will immediately hit the same serialization error... but we can still recover and write to something the first transaction didn't touch.
